---
import core from '../../../lib/relational-collection/core.json';
import './modelling.css';
const product = core.products.find(p => p.product_id === 'P7')!;
const line = core.order_items.find(i => i.order_id === 103 && i.line_no === 1)!;
---
<figure class="r01-figure" aria-labelledby="r01-price-title">
  <div class="r01-head"><span>Figure 3</span><p id="r01-price-title">Two prices that are allowed to disagree</p></div>
  <div class="r01-prices">
    <section><p class="r01-panel-title">Product P7</p><p>Current catalogue price</p><div class="r01-price-change"><span>{product.price}<small>At purchase</small></span><span aria-label="changes to">→</span><span>25.00<small>21 October, 09:00 UTC</small></span></div></section>
    <section><p class="r01-panel-title">Order 103 · line 1</p><p>Agreed unit price</p><div class="r01-price-change"><span>{line.unit_price}<small>At purchase</small></span><span aria-label="remains">→</span><span>{line.unit_price}<small>Still owed for this mug</small></span></div></section>
  </div>
  <figcaption>P7’s catalogue price changes on 21 October 2026. The existing order line keeps its agreed 20.00. These are different facts; their earlier equality did not make one a redundant copy of the other.</figcaption>
</figure>
